{
  "requirement": "The `habeas` CLI MUST use `clap` to define and parse its commands and options.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Judgment: `requirements.cli.clap` is satisfied.** The CLI is defined and parsed with `clap`, the implementation is marked, and the tests cover the main cases.\n\n**Implementation**\n- `Cargo.toml:55` depends on `clap` 4.6.7 with the `derive` feature.\n- `src/main.rs` defines the whole CLI with clap derive types:\n  - `Cli` (`#[derive(Parser)]`, name `habeas`, line 10) is the entry type.\n  - `Command` (`#[derive(Subcommand)]`, line 18) holds the top-level commands: `mdbook`, `setup`, `status`, `info`, `author` (with its `author-evidence` alias), the hidden `hook`, and `add-input`.\n  - `MdbookCommand` (line 87) and `HookCommand` (line 93) hold the nested subcommands.\n  - Options and argument groups are declared with `#[arg]`, `ArgGroup` and `conflicts_with`.\n- `main` calls `Cli::parse()` (line 100), which is the only parse entry point.\n- All five items carry `{impl}` markers for `requirements.cli.clap`. A search of `src` found no `env::args` or other manual argument parsing.\n- The other `{impl}` markers inside these items point to other requirements that specify the behavior behind the commands. They do not leave unmarked behavior for this requirement.\n\n**Tests**\n- `tests/cli.rs::clap_defines_commands_options_and_usage_errors` runs the real binary and checks the following:\n  - `--help` output starts with `Usage: habeas <COMMAND>` and lists the visible commands, including `add-input`.\n  - The hidden `hook` command does not appear in help.\n  - `status --help` shows `--all` and `--verbose`.\n  - The `author-evidence` alias resolves to `habeas author`.\n  - Usage errors exit with code 2 and print the usage line. The cases are an unknown flag, a missing required argument, and the conflicting `author x --all`.\n- `tests/commands.rs::add_input_requires_one_target_and_mode_and_resets_changed_records` also carries a `{test}` marker. It checks the clap `ArgGroup` rules for `add-input`: missing or multiple targets or modes exit with code 2 and show `Usage: habeas add-input`.\n- Together these tests cover the parsing, grouping, alias, hidden-command and failure cases.",
